<ArticleLayout
  title="What Is a Healthy BMI? Chart by Age, Gender & Height | Helthvibe"
  description="What BMI is considered healthy? See charts broken down by age, gender, height, and ethnicity, with the limits of a single healthy BMI number."
  h1="What Is a Healthy BMI? Chart by Age, Gender & Height"
  excerpt="A single healthy BMI number doesn't fit everyone. Here's how age, gender, height, and ethnicity shift the optimal range."
  datePublished="2026-09-27"
  dateModified="2026-09-27"
  category="Reference"
  readTime="9 min"
  showCalculator={true}
  faqs={faqs}
  related={related}
>
  <p>
    The World Health Organization defines a healthy adult BMI as 18.5 to 24.9. That's the number most guidelines use.
    But it isn't the same number for everyone — a healthy BMI shifts with age, gender, ethnicity, and what we're using the
    number to predict.
  </p>

  <p>
    Below, we walk through the standard healthy range, then break it down by age group, by gender, by ethnicity (with the
    WHO-aligned Asian cutoffs), and by height. We close with how to think about BMI as one of several health signals,
    rather than a verdict.
  </p>

  <h2>The standard healthy BMI range</h2>
  <p>
    The WHO adult BMI classification, in widespread use globally since the 1990s, defines:
  </p>

  <table>
    <caption>WHO adult BMI classification</caption>
    <thead><tr><th>BMI</th><th>Category</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Below 18.5</td><td>Underweight</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>18.5 – 24.9</strong></td><td><strong>Healthy range</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td>25.0 – 29.9</td><td>Overweight</td></tr>
      <tr><td>30.0 and above</td><td>Obese</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    Most clinical guidelines — including the CDC in the US, NHS in the UK, and national bodies across Europe, Asia, and
    Oceania — use this same band. If you've ever checked your BMI and gotten a "you're in the healthy range" message, this
    is the band it's referring to.
  </p>

  <h2>Healthy BMI by age group</h2>
  <p>
    For children and teens (ages 2–19), BMI is interpreted as a percentile against age- and sex-matched peers, not as a
    fixed category. A BMI at the 85th percentile or higher is "overweight"; at the 95th percentile or higher is "obese."
  </p>

  <p>
    For adults, the standard 18.5–24.9 range holds steady from age 20 through 64. After 65, the picture changes — see
    below.
  </p>

  <h3>Adults 65 and older</h3>
  <p>
    Several large studies suggest that older adults fare better with a BMI slightly above the standard range. A 2014
    meta-analysis in the <em>American Journal of Clinical Nutrition</em> found that adults over 65 with a BMI between
    22 and 27 had the lowest all-cause mortality. Below 22, mortality risk rose — likely reflecting malnutrition, sarcopenia,
    or chronic illness.
  </p>

  <p>
    This doesn't mean "more weight is always better" for seniors. It means the lower bound of "healthy" likely shifts up
    for older adults. Our [Senior BMI calculator](/calculators/bmi-seniors) uses 22–27 as the healthy range for adults 65+.
  </p>

  <h3>Children and teens (ages 2–19)</h3>
  <p>
    Pediatric BMI uses age- and sex-specific percentiles, calculated against CDC growth charts:
  </p>

  <table>
    <caption>CDC BMI percentile classification (ages 2–19)</caption>
    <thead><tr><th>Percentile</th><th>Category</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Below the 5th percentile</td><td>Underweight</td></tr>
      <tr><td>5th to 84th percentile</td><td>Healthy weight</td></tr>
      <tr><td>85th to 94th percentile</td><td>Overweight</td></tr>
      <tr><td>95th percentile and above</td><td>Obese</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    Our [Child & Teen BMI calculator](/calculators/bmi-children) computes this percentile for you.
  </p>

  <h2>Healthy BMI by gender</h2>
  <p>
    The WHO cutoffs are the same for men and women — but body composition differs in ways that matter.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Women</strong> carry a higher percentage of essential fat (12% vs. 3% in men). A given BMI in the healthy range typically reflects a higher body-fat percentage for women than for men.</li>
    <li><strong>Men</strong> tend to carry weight around the abdomen; <strong>women</strong> tend to carry it around the hips and thighs. Abdominal (visceral) fat carries higher cardiometabolic risk.</li>
    <li><strong>Waist circumference</strong> is therefore a useful complement to BMI: above 40 in (102 cm) for men or 35 in (88 cm) for women signals elevated risk regardless of BMI.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    For a BMI calculator that adds female-specific context, see our [Women BMI calculator](/calculators/bmi-women).
    For male-specific context, see [BMI for Men](/calculators/bmi-men).
  </p>

  <h2>Healthy BMI by ethnicity</h2>
  <p>
    Different ethnic groups develop weight-related health complications at different BMIs. The strongest and most studied
    case is Asian populations, where the WHO has issued adjusted cutoffs.
  </p>

  <h3>Asian populations</h3>
  <p>
    Studies in the 1990s and 2000s showed that South, East, and Southeast Asian populations tend to develop type 2 diabetes,
    hypertension, and cardiovascular disease at BMIs well below the standard 25 cutoff. The WHO recommends lower thresholds:
  </p>

  <table>
    <caption>WHO Asian-adjusted BMI classification</caption>
    <thead><tr><th>BMI</th><th>Category</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Below 18.5</td><td>Underweight</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>18.5 – 22.9</strong></td><td><strong>Healthy range</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td>23.0 – 27.4</td><td>Overweight</td></tr>
      <tr><td>27.5 and above</td><td>Obese</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    A BMI of 24, "healthy" by the standard chart, would be classified as overweight for an Asian adult. Use our
    [Asian BMI calculator](/calculators/bmi-asian) with the Asian-threshold toggle to see both ranges side by side.
  </p>

  <h3>Other ethnic considerations</h3>
  <p>
    Research is ongoing for Black, Hispanic, and other populations. Some studies suggest that the standard cutoffs may also
    misclassify risk in these groups, though no consensus has produced new official cutoffs. A clinician who knows your
    personal and family history can give you better guidance than a single number.
  </p>

  <h2>Healthy BMI by height</h2>
  <p>
    BMI is calculated from height and weight, but the "healthy" range itself doesn't change by height — 18.5 to 24.9
    applies to a person who is 5'2" (157 cm) just as it does to someone who is 6'3" (191 cm).
  </p>

  <p>
    What does change is the absolute weight that corresponds to that BMI. The healthy weight range for different heights:
  </p>

  <table>
    <caption>Healthy weight range for common heights (BMI 18.5–24.9)</caption>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Height</th><th>Healthy weight range (kg)</th><th>Healthy weight range (lb)</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>5'0" (152 cm)</td><td>42.8 – 57.6</td><td>94 – 127</td></tr>
      <tr><td>5'2" (157 cm)</td><td>45.6 – 61.4</td><td>100 – 135</td></tr>
      <tr><td>5'4" (163 cm)</td><td>49.1 – 66.1</td><td>108 – 146</td></tr>
      <tr><td>5'6" (168 cm)</td><td>52.2 – 70.3</td><td>115 – 155</td></tr>
      <tr><td>5'8" (173 cm)</td><td>55.4 – 74.6</td><td>122 – 164</td></tr>
      <tr><td>5'10" (178 cm)</td><td>58.6 – 78.9</td><td>129 – 174</td></tr>
      <tr><td>6'0" (183 cm)</td><td>62.0 – 83.4</td><td>137 – 184</td></tr>
      <tr><td>6'2" (188 cm)</td><td>65.4 – 88.1</td><td>144 – 194</td></tr>
      <tr><td>6'4" (193 cm)</td><td>68.9 – 92.8</td><td>152 – 205</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    Use our [Healthy Weight calculator](/calculators/healthy-weight) for a customized version based on your exact height.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the "healthy range" is a range, not a single number</h2>
  <p>
    The 18.5–24.9 band is a population-level statistical range, not a personalized target. Within that range, there's no
    evidence that 21.5 is "healthier" than 23.5. The body of epidemiological research shows roughly the lowest mortality risk
    across most of the band, with a slight U-shape — risk rises slightly at both ends.
  </p>

  <p>
    So if you're at 19.5 or 24.5, you're not at the "edge" of healthy in any meaningful sense. You're in the range.
    Worrying about a number within the band is rarely useful.
  </p>

  <h2>Beyond BMI: other health signals</h2>
  <p>
    A growing body of research suggests that several other measurements predict health risk as well as, or better than,
    BMI:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Waist-to-height ratio:</strong> divide your waist circumference by your height. A ratio below 0.5 is generally considered low risk; above 0.5 is elevated.</li>
    <li><strong>Body-fat percentage:</strong> a more direct measure of adiposity, though methods vary in accuracy.</li>
    <li><strong>Waist circumference alone:</strong> above 40 in (102 cm) for men or 35 in (88 cm) for women signals elevated cardiometabolic risk.</li>
    <li><strong>Bloodwork:</strong> fasting glucose, HbA1c, lipid panel, and blood pressure are far better predictors of cardiometabolic disease than BMI alone.</li>
    <li><strong>Cardiorespiratory fitness:</strong> "Fit and fat" people consistently have lower mortality than "unfit and thin" people, even at the same BMI.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    See our [BMI vs. body-fat guide](/articles/bmi-vs-body-fat-percentage) and [Is BMI Accurate in 2026?](/articles/is-bmi-accurate-in-2026)
    for more on this.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>The takeaway:</strong> The 18.5–24.9 BMI band is a useful population-level signal, but it isn't a personal
    target. The healthiest BMI for <em>you</em> depends on your age, ethnicity, body composition, fitness, and overall
    health profile. Use BMI as one input — not the only one.
  </div>
</ArticleLayout>
